{"id": "ku_8b9e1e00e5", "claim": "So “all coming into my mind at once” is rendered more efficiently and perhaps more elegantly as “thronging.” The images of the soldiers’ past activ- ities charge into sight, as if into battle. What is more, the subsequent phrase that is translated “at one and the same time”—eis hena kairon—might per- haps be better rendered “on this one occasion,” to get at the rhetorical effect of Demosthenes’ particularly cunning move.", "sources": [{"chunk_id": "unknown", "doc_id": "8d7f377c4fe57fa8", "pages": [], "author": "Hawhee, Debra"}], "confidence": 0.81, "domain": "rickert", "created_from_query": "attunement and ambient persuasion"}
